From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kevin Turcios <turcioskevinr@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 04:33:43 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Optimization: Index tuples by constants directly

"x[2]" with a constant index read lists and strings directly, but sent
tuples through the generic subscript slot, which turns the index back
into an integer object first. Tuples, and subclasses that do not
override "__getitem__", e.g. named tuples, are now read directly too,
with the same errors.

A loop of constant subscripts of a tuple subclass takes 5.0 instead of
6.6 ms, where CPython 3.14 takes 9.8 ms.

Co-authored-by: wtfyd-agent[bot] <328989958+wtfyd-agent[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>
Signed-off-by: Kevin Turcios <turcioskevinr@gmail.com>
---
 .../build/include/nuitka/helper/subscripts.h  | 24 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+)

diff --git a/nuitka/build/include/nuitka/helper/subscripts.h b/nuitka/build/include/nuitka/helper/subscripts.h
index 596bfee09..2d11f3898 100644
--- a/nuitka/build/include/nuitka/helper/subscripts.h
+++ b/nuitka/build/include/nuitka/helper/subscripts.h
@@ -106,6 +106,30 @@ NUITKA_MAY_BE_UNUSED static PyObject *LOOKUP_SUBSCRIPT_CONST(PyThreadState *tsta
 
             result = ((PyListObject *)source)->ob_item[int_subscript];
 
+            Py_INCREF(result);
+            return result;
+        } else if (tp_as_mapping->mp_subscript == PyTuple_Type.tp_as_mapping->mp_subscript) {
+            // Tuples, and subclasses that inherit tuple's subscript, e.g. named
+            // tuples, which are indexed by constants a lot.
+            assert(PyTuple_Check(source));
+            Py_ssize_t tuple_size = PyTuple_GET_SIZE(source);
+
+            if (int_subscript < 0) {
+                if (-int_subscript > tuple_size) {
+                    SET_CURRENT_EXCEPTION_TYPE0_STR(tstate, PyExc_IndexError, "tuple index out of range");
+                    return NULL;
+                }
+
+                int_subscript += tuple_size;
+            } else {
+                if (int_subscript >= tuple_size) {
+                    SET_CURRENT_EXCEPTION_TYPE0_STR(tstate, PyExc_IndexError, "tuple index out of range");
+                    return NULL;
+                }
+            }
+
+            result = PyTuple_GET_ITEM(source, int_subscript);
+
             Py_INCREF(result);
             return result;
         }
